Here's a short summary of the journey so far:

- **Built the foundation in Selenium.** We automated the BISS iNet journeys, ENTS transfers, Appeals and the Staff portal in Cucumber BDD. Everything goes through one shared action engine (`iAction`) and a single Object Repository.
- **Moved the engine to Playwright without touching the tests.** The step definitions, feature files and locator keys stayed exactly as they were. Underneath, chromedriver and the hand-written waits are gone, replaced by auto-waiting and tracing.
- **Made the tests stable on Angular.** We built self-healing handling for dropdowns, spinners, dialogs and click fallbacks into the engine. Fixing an issue once fixes it for every test case.
- **Solved test data, our biggest pain point.** Test data now comes from Oracle at run time instead of herds typed into Excel. A pre-run harvest (TC_00_DATA) and a Test Data Vault track each herd as available, reserved, used or bad, so the weekly CENTEST refresh no longer breaks the runs.
- **Kept farmer data and secrets safe.** Feature files hold no herd numbers. The vault is never committed, reports mask herds and logins, and credentials live only in an OpenShift secret.
- **Put it on CI/CD.** A Jenkins multibranch pipeline now runs nightly on OpenShift, using a container image with the browsers built in.
- **Made the results visible.** Every run produces step-level logs, Word reports, a Test Dashboard for the team, a stakeholder PDF, and a trace for any failed step.
- **Built it to scale.** New portals, data pools, browsers and team members all land on the same core. The next steps are parallel packs and the remaining Staff test cases (TC_28 to TC_37).